\section{Introduction}\label{sec:cap-intro}

Memory safety bugs---buffer overflows, use-after-free errors, null pointer
dereferences, and type confusions---remain the most common class of security
vulnerabilities in systems software.  Despite decades of research on static
analysis, type systems, and runtime checks, these bugs persist because the
underlying machine model provides no hardware enforcement of memory safety.
Pointers are just integers; any code that can compute a pointer can dereference
it, regardless of whether it has the right to do so.

\emph{Capability-based security} offers a fundamentally different approach.  A
\emph{capability} is an unforgeable token that grants its holder the right to
access a specific region of memory in a specific way.  Unlike pointers,
capabilities cannot be fabricated from thin air; they can only be derived from
existing capabilities through controlled operations.  This provides
\emph{hardware-enforced} memory safety: even if a program contains a bug, the
bug cannot lead to a memory safety violation because the hardware prevents
invalid memory accesses.

The CHERI (Capability Hardware Enhanced RISC Instructions)
project~\cite{watson2015cheri,watson2020cheri} has demonstrated that
capability-based security can be implemented efficiently in modern processors.
CHERI extends the MIPS and Arm architectures with hardware capabilities,
providing fine-grained memory protection without the overhead of software-based
approaches.

This chapter explores the design of intermediate representations that support
capability-based security (\cref{sec:cap-ir}), the compilation of high-level
languages to capability machines (\cref{sec:cap-compilation}), and the security
guarantees that capabilities provide (\cref{sec:cap-guarantees}).

% ============================================================
% Capabilities and Memory Safety
% ============================================================
\section{Capabilities and Memory Safety}\label{sec:cap-memory-safety}

\subsection{The Capability Model}\label{sec:cap-model}

\begin{definition}[Capability]\label{def:capability}
A \emph{capability} is a tuple $(b, l, p, a, t)$ where:
\begin{itemize}
\item $b$ is the \emph{base address} (the start of the permitted region).
\item $l$ is the \emph{length} (the size of the permitted region).
\item $p$ is the set of \emph{permissions} (read, write, execute, etc.).
\item $a$ is the \emph{address} (the current position within the region).
\item $t$ is a \emph{tag bit} indicating whether the capability is valid.
\end{itemize}
A capability is \emph{in bounds} if $b \leq a < b + l$.  A capability is
\emph{authorized} for an operation if the tag bit is set and the permission for
that operation is granted.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Capability Operations}\label{sec:cap-operations}

The hardware provides a set of operations on capabilities:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Load/store through capability:} Dereference a capability to read
      or write memory.  The hardware checks bounds and permissions.
\item \textbf{Derive capability:} Create a new capability by narrowing an
      existing one (reducing bounds, removing permissions).  This is the
      \emph{monotonicity} property: capabilities can only be narrowed, never
      widened.
\item \textbf{Seal/unseal:} Mark a capability as opaque (sealed) so that it
      cannot be dereferenced or derived until it is unsealed with the
      appropriate key.  This supports type-safe dispatch and object encapsulation.
\item \textbf{Test and query:} Check whether a capability is valid, in bounds,
      or has specific permissions.
\end{itemize}

\begin{definition}[Monotonicity]\label{def:monotonicity}
The \emph{monotonicity} property of capabilities states that if capability $c'$
is derived from capability $c$, then:
\[
[b', b'+l') \subseteq [b, b+l) \quad \text{and} \quad p' \subseteq p
\]
That is, derived capabilities can only refer to sub-regions with fewer
permissions than their parents.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Memory Safety Guarantees}\label{sec:cap-guarantee}

Capabilities provide strong memory safety guarantees:

\begin{theorem}[Spatial Safety]\label{thm:spatial-safety}
On a capability machine, every memory access through a valid capability is
within the bounds of that capability.  Therefore, buffer overflows and
underflows are impossible (assuming all memory is accessed through capabilities).
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Temporal Safety (with revocation)]\label{thm:temporal-safety}
If capabilities are revoked when memory is deallocated (e.g., by clearing tag
bits), then use-after-free errors are impossible.
\end{theorem}

