\section{IR Support for Capabilities}\label{sec:cap-ir}

\subsection{Extending the IR}\label{sec:cap-ir-extend}

To compile to capability machines, the intermediate representation must support
capability types and operations.  The key extensions are:

\begin{definition}[Capability-Aware IR]\label{def:cap-ir}
A \emph{capability-aware IR} extends the base IR with:
\begin{itemize}
\item A \emph{capability type} $\mathit{cap}(T)$ representing a capability to
      memory of type $T$.
\item \emph{Capability operations}: $\mathit{derive}$, $\mathit{seal}$,
      $\mathit{unseal}$, $\mathit{bounds}$, $\mathit{perms}$.
\item \emph{Capability-typed memory operations}: loads and stores that take
      capabilities as address operands.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Type-Based Capability Derivation}\label{sec:cap-derive}

In a high-level language with a type system, the compiler can automatically
derive capabilities from type information:

\begin{itemize}
\item A pointer to a struct field is derived by narrowing the struct's
      capability to the field's offset and size.
\item An array element access is derived by narrowing the array's capability
      to the element's offset and size.
\item A function pointer is derived by narrowing the code segment's capability
      to the function's address range and adding execute permission.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[Automatic Capability Derivation]\label{ex:cap-derive}
Consider the C code:
\begin{verbatim}
  struct Point { int x; int y; };
  struct Point p;
  p.x = 10;
\end{verbatim}
In a capability-aware IR, the address of \texttt{p.x} is computed by deriving
a capability from \texttt{\&p}:
\[
\mathit{cap\_x} = \mathit{derive}(\mathit{cap\_p}, \mathit{offset} = 0, \mathit{len} = 4, \mathit{perms} = \{\mathit{RW}\})
\]
The store \texttt{p.x = 10} then uses $\mathit{cap\_x}$ as the address operand.
\end{example}

\subsection{CheriBSD and the CHERI LLVM Fork}\label{sec:cap-llvm}

The CHERI project includes a fork of LLVM that compiles C/C++ to CHERI
capability code.  The compiler automatically inserts capability derivation
instructions, ensuring that all memory accesses are bounds-checked by the
hardware.  CheriBSD~\cite{cherebsd2020} is a FreeBSD derivative that runs
entirely in capability mode, providing system-wide memory safety.

