\section{Compiling to Capability Machines}\label{sec:cap-compilation}

\subsection{The Compilation Challenge}\label{sec:cap-challenge}

Compiling to capability machines presents unique challenges:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Capability provenance:} Every capability must be derived from a
      valid parent capability.  The compiler must track the provenance of all
      capabilities.
\item \textbf{Representable bounds:} The hardware's compressed bounds
      representation means that not all bounds are exactly representable.  The
      compiler must round bounds to representable values, potentially granting
      more access than necessary.
\item \textbf{Register pressure:} Capabilities are larger than pointers (128--256
      bits vs.\ 64 bits), increasing register pressure.
\item \textbf{Calling conventions:} Function calls must pass capabilities
      correctly, including sealing and unsealing for type safety.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{CheriABI}\label{sec:cap-abi}

The CHERI ABI defines how capabilities are used in the calling convention,
memory layout, and system interface:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Pure-capability mode:} All pointers are capabilities; the integer
      address space is not used for memory access.
\item \textbf{Hybrid mode:} Capabilities are used selectively; integer pointers
      are used for legacy code.
\item \textbf{Capability registers:} The first $N$ arguments are passed in
      capability registers; the rest on the capability-aware stack.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Stack Frame Layout}\label{sec:cap-stack}

On a capability machine, each stack frame contains capabilities rather than
raw pointers.  The frame pointer, return address, and local variable addresses
are all capabilities with bounds restricted to the current frame.

