\section{Security Guarantees}\label{sec:cap-guarantees}

\subsection{Confinement}\label{sec:cap-confinement}

Capabilities enable \emph{confinement}: restricting a component's access to
only the memory it needs.  This is the foundation of sandboxing on capability
machines.

\begin{definition}[Confinement]\label{def:confinement}
A component $C$ is \emph{confined} if the only memory it can access is the
memory covered by the capabilities it holds.  If $C$ is given a capability with
bounds $[b, b+l)$ and permissions $p$, then $C$ cannot access any memory outside
$[b, b+l)$ or perform any operation not in $p$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Compartmentalization}\label{sec:cap-compartment}

\emph{Software compartmentalization} divides a program into isolated
compartments, each with its own set of capabilities.  Communication between
compartments is mediated by the runtime, which passes capabilities across
compartment boundaries.

The CHERI project has demonstrated compartmentalization of large applications
(including the Nginx web server and the FreeBSD kernel), showing that
compartmentalization can be applied to real-world software with acceptable
performance overhead.

\subsection{Forward-Edge and Backward-Edge CFI}\label{sec:cap-cfi}

Capabilities provide strong control-flow integrity (CFI) guarantees:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Forward-edge CFI:} Function pointers are sealed capabilities that
      can only be invoked through the \texttt{CJump} instruction.  This prevents
      attackers from redirecting control flow to arbitrary code.
\item \textbf{Backward-edge CFI:} Return addresses are sealed capabilities on
      the stack.  An attacker who overwrites the stack cannot forge a valid
      return capability, preventing return-oriented programming (ROP) attacks.
\end{itemize}

\begin{theorem}[CFI Guarantee]\label{thm:cfi}
On a CHERI processor running CHERI-compiled code, control-flow hijacking attacks
(ROP, COF, jump-oriented programming) are impossible, because:
\begin{enumerate}
\item All function pointers are sealed capabilities that cannot be forged.
\item Return addresses are sealed capabilities that cannot be forged.
\item The tag bit system prevents capabilities from being fabricated from
      integer data.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

