\section{Applications to Sandboxing}\label{sec:cap-sandbox}

\subsection{WebAssembly on CHERI}\label{sec:cap-wasm}

WebAssembly (Wasm) is a sandboxed bytecode format for the web.  Running Wasm on
a CHERI processor provides hardware-enforced sandboxing: each Wasm instance
receives a capability to its linear memory, and all memory accesses are
bounds-checked by the hardware.  This eliminates the need for software bounds
checks, improving performance by 10--30\% compared to software sandboxing.

\subsection{Library Sandboxing}\label{sec:cap-library}

Capabilities can be used to sandbox individual libraries within a process.
Each library receives capabilities only to its own data and to the shared data
it needs.  If the library contains a vulnerability, the attacker cannot access
memory outside the library's capabilities.

\subsection{Microkernels}\label{sec:cap-microkernel}

Capability-based microkernels (e.g., seL4~\cite{klein2009sel4} adapted to
CHERI) use capabilities as the fundamental access control mechanism.  Each
kernel object (thread, channel, memory region) is associated with a capability,
and all access is mediated by the hardware.

\begin{historical}
The concept of capabilities dates back to the Calico operating system
(Brown University, 1970s) and the CAP computer (Cambridge, 1970s).  The idea
lay dormant for decades, superseded by page-based virtual memory.  The CHERI
project~\cite{watson2015cheri}, launched in 2010 at the University of Cambridge
and SRI International, revived capability hardware for the modern era,
demonstrating that capabilities can be implemented efficiently in a
production-quality RISC processor.  The CHERI-Morello prototype
(2020)~\cite{watson2020cheri} demonstrated capability hardware in an Arm-based
processor, paving the way for commercial adoption.
\end{historical}

