\section{Comparison with Software Approaches}\label{sec:cap-comparison}

\subsection{Capabilities vs.\ Software Checks}\label{sec:cap-vs-sw}

Software-based memory safety approaches---bounds checking, garbage collection,
safe languages (Rust, Java)---provide memory safety through software mechanisms.
Capability hardware provides the same guarantees through hardware enforcement.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\caption{Hardware Capabilities vs.\ Software Safety Mechanisms}\label{tab:cap-vs-sw}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\hline
\textbf{Aspect} & \textbf{Capabilities} & \textbf{Software Checks} \\
\hline
Performance overhead & 5--15\% & 10--50\% \\
Safety guarantee & Hardware-enforced & Depends on implementation \\
Retrofittable & Yes (CHERI) & Varies \\
Granularity & Per-object & Per-check \\
Language support & Any C/C++ & Language-specific \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Migration Path}\label{sec:cap-migration}

The CHERI project provides a practical migration path from conventional code to
capability-secured code:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Hybrid mode:} Compile with both integer pointers and capabilities.
      Legacy code uses integer pointers; new code uses capabilities.
\item \textbf{Pure-capability mode:} Compile all code in capability mode.
      All pointers become capabilities.
\item \textbf{Full sandboxing:} Use capabilities for compartmentalization,
      restricting each component's access to its own data.
\end{enumerate}

