% Summary
% ============================================================
\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:cap-summary}

Capability-secured IR represents a paradigm shift in how compilers think about
memory safety.  Rather than relying on static analysis or runtime checks,
capability-based compilation provides hardware-enforced safety guarantees that
hold even in the presence of bugs.

Key open challenges include:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Performance optimization:} Reducing the overhead of capability
      operations (derivation, sealing, tag checking).
\item \textbf{Language integration:} Designing programming language type systems
      that map naturally to capabilities.
\item \textbf{Temporal safety:} Extending capabilities to prevent use-after-free
      (requires hardware support for capability revocation).
\item \textbf{Adoption:} Building the ecosystem (compilers, libraries, OS
      support) needed for capability hardware to reach mainstream use.
\end{itemize}

The CHERI project's web site\footnote{\url{https://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/research/security/ctsrd/cheri/}}
provides extensive documentation, including the CHERI ISA specification, the
LLVM fork, and CheriBSD source code.
